# Rank 1865 / CVE-2017-15010: tough-cookie's cookie pair regex let a space
# run be redistributed between two quantifiers that both match whitespace, so
# a cookie header built from a name, 65535 spaces and no `=` cost quadratic
# backtracking in Cookie.parse (CWE-1333 inefficient regular expression
# complexity, CWE-400).
#
# COOKIE_PAIR and LOOSE_COOKIE_PAIR (lib/cookie.js:61 and :65 at the fixed revision
# parent) are /^(([^=;]+))\s*=\s*([^\n\r\0]*)/ and
# /^((?:=)?([^=;]*)\s*=\s*)?([^\n\r\0]*)/. The group's trailing atom [^=;]+
# admits every whitespace character, and the \s* that follows it matches
# whitespace too, so on a failing subject the engine can hand each space to
# either quantifier and walks the O(n^2) set of splits; the advisory's own
# regression test measures the broken form at 2000-4000x the bounded one.
# parse (lib/cookie.js:320) applies the literal through a ternary-selected
# module constant: `var pairRe = options.loose ? LOOSE_COOKIE_PAIR :
# COOKIE_PAIR; var result = pairRe.exec(firstSemi === -1 ? str :
# str.substr(0,firstSemi));`. The fixed revision f1ed420a bounds the second quantifier
# to \s{0,256} in both literals and changes nothing else, so the whole
# discriminator is the quantifier bound and it lives in the pattern text, at
# module scope, never at the call site.
#
# The pool row carries no flag and reads correctly: f1ed420a is the targeted
# fix touching lib/cookie.js plus its regression test, the vulnerable
# literals are present at the parent, and the remediation is present at the
# commit. .
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap, and four stacked absences block it.
#
# First, the shared cost model answers false, and correctly for what it
# models: regexambig.Ambiguous answers false for this pattern because it has
# no *nested* overlapping quantified branch (no (a+)+ shape), and polynomial
# backtracking is a class the engine does not model -- the same boundary rank
# 1343 recorded for loofah's quadratic url() substitution, where any
# shippable form "needs an operand-boundedness notion alongside a polynomial
# cost model".
#
# Second, the JavaScript frontend has its own adjacent-quantifier analysis
# for exactly this class -- hasAmbiguousAdjacentRegexQuantifiers feeds
# jsRegexMayBacktrack (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go:1861)
# -- but its overlap test regexAtomsOverlap (javascript.go:2124) is bare
# string equality: `a == b || a == "any" || b == "any"`. [^=;] and \s whose
# character classes intersect on whitespace are never recognised as
# overlapping, so even the ungrouped spelling /^x[^=;]+\s*=/ leaves the
# literal a plain Const and synthesises no __regex.match node.
#
# Third, jsRegexAtoms (javascript.go:1924) lowers a group to an opaque atom
# keyed "group" carrying only the quantifier *after* the closing paren, so
# overlap across a group boundary is invisible even for identically-spelled
# atoms: /^\s+\s*=/ synthesises a __regex.match node and /^(\s+)\s*=/, one
# paren pair later, does not. This CVE's first quantifier sits two groups
# deep.
#
# Fourth, even where the analysis does fire the synthesised __regex.match
# node (javascript.go:3579) has no JavaScript binding: Ruby's regexMatch205
# labels its node code.RegexMatch and code.RegexMatchSink, and the
# JavaScript bindings carry no __regex consumer -- the served routes are the
# call-site literal-argument needles (regexMatch540, regexMatch970) and five
# per-CVE literal fragments (catastrophicRegex541..546). /\033\[(\d+)*m/.exec
# (s), a nested-ambiguity literal applied exactly as tough-cookie applies
# its pair regex, scans to zero findings for the same reason. So fixing the
# cost model alone would still report nothing through the receiver-applied
# route this code uses.
#
# A binding cannot bridge it instead, measured three ways. The pattern text
# the fixed revision changes is \s*=, and that is the universal key-value delimiter
# idiom: across the sibling checkouts it appears in regex literals in ten
# files -- growi's bootstrap migration alone carries dozens of distinct
# class\s*=\s*['"] attribute scanners, geoxml3's KML parsers, highlight.pack,
# bokeh -- with one real among them, reproducing rank 1343's one-in-seven-to-
# eleven measurement of exactly this route. A sink on the application is
# worse: .exec( and .test( occur at 1198 call sites across 390 sibling files
# (and .exec collides with child_process.exec), and the call site is
# character-for-character identical at both revisions of this repository, so
# no call-site label can discriminate. Linking the receiver to its module
# literal has no route either -- JavaScript emits no call-argument tokens and
# no assign: token from a variable_declarator, and the module-scope literal
# is the comment-silenceable text route the ten-file census prices above.
# Concepts considered and unreachable: code.CatastrophicRegex (the ungated
# presence route the census kills), code.CatastrophicRegexSink,
# code.RegexMatchSink (needs a call-site label), code.RegexMatch (the Ruby
# synthesis label). Nothing minted.
#
# Measured on the real checkout, both revisions, timeout 180 --max-ram 6GB
# --cache off, three runs per revision at the current definitions state: 12
# findings at the vulnerable revision and 12 at the fixed revision (5
# VYQL-INJ-013 prototype-pollution rows on memstore.js and the JSON tests, 5
# VYQL-INJ-002 rows, 1 VYQL-INJ-134, 1 VYQL-CRY-003), byte-stable inside each
# revision and identical as rule-and-sink fingerprint multisets across the
# revision boundary, zero discrimination in both directions; the review-flag
# view holds 1 flag per revision, the same NestedPathInheritedProperty
# PrototypePollution on lib/memstore.js:110. Nothing is DOS-related; the only
# label anywhere on the parse is code.CommandExecution on pairRe.exec
# (lib/cookie.js:330, one of the five INJ-002 rows), the known regex .exec
# mislabel, present character-for-character at both revisions, plus library
# param-source taint findings unrelated to the pair regex. vyql match on the
# real parse shape reports no source and no sink for VYQL-DOS-002's pair;
# vyql trace from code.ExternalEntryInput to code.RegexMatchSink over the
# repro reports 0 sources, 0 dead-ends.
#
# Recheck at the current definitions state, engine asked directly: the
# shared ambiguity module has drifted since the original measurement in two
# ways -- regexBodyHasRepeat now skips [...] class spans, and
# regexSplitsOneCharRun accepts a second consumer at the head of an
# unquantified group's branch -- and neither reaches this class, because the
# run-split shape stays restricted to a single-character first set while
# [^=;]+ is a wide negated class, by the recorded precision decision that
# wide-class repeats are ordinary. jsRegexMayBacktrack answers false for
# both vulnerable literals and for the bounded fixed one, true for the
# nested-quantifier ansi pattern and for the ungrouped identically-spelled
# /^\s+\s*=/, false again for /^(\s+)\s*=/ one paren pair later, and
# regexAtomsOverlap("[^=;]", "\\s") is false while ("\\s", "\\s") is true:
# the four stacked absences above all hold, no JavaScript binding consumes
# __regex.match, and vyql graph on the vulnerable lib/cookie.js synthesises
# zero __regex.match nodes.
#
# This is not a mechanical fix: making regexAtomsOverlap compare character
# classes, teaching jsRegexAtoms to see a group's trailing atom, or adding a
# polynomial case to the shared regexambig cost model are each a new
# classification with scan-wide effect on what gets labelled, and 1343's
# measurement of the candidate shapes says the obvious forms are not
# reportable without the operand-boundedness notion. The pair below pins the
# boundary with no tough-cookie API: the same exported function applying a
# pattern to its parameter reports VYQL-DOS-002 when the literal carries a
# nested ambiguous quantifier and reports nothing when it carries this CVE's
# adjacent-overlap literal instead -- the two differ only in the quantifier
# structure of the pattern.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.
# When a polynomial backtracking classification lands, the first test flips
# to expect VYQL-DOS-003 and the second stays reject.

test "rank1864: the cookie pair regex splits a space run between overlapping quantifiers"
  lang javascript
  file lib/cookie.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  var COOKIE_PAIR = /^(([^=;]+))\s*=\s*([^\n\r\0]*)/;

  var LOOSE_COOKIE_PAIR = /^((?:=)?([^=;]*)\s*=\s*)?([^\n\r\0]*)/;

  function Cookie() {}

  function parse(str, options) {
    if (!options || typeof options !== 'object') {
      options = {};
    }
    str = str.trim();

    var firstSemi = str.indexOf(';');
    var pairRe = options.loose ? LOOSE_COOKIE_PAIR : COOKIE_PAIR;
    var result = pairRe.exec(firstSemi === -1 ? str : str.substr(0,firstSemi));

    if (!result) {
      return;
    }

    var c = new Cookie();
    c.key = result[2].trim();
    c.value = result[3].trim();
    return c;
  }

  module.exports = {
    Cookie: Cookie,
    parse: parse
  };
  ```

test "rank1864: bounding the space run before the separator keeps the parse linear"
  lang javascript
  file lib/cookie.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  var COOKIE_PAIR = /^(([^=;]+))\s{0,256}=\s*([^\n\r\0]*)/;

  var LOOSE_COOKIE_PAIR = /^((?:=)?([^=;]*)\s{0,256}=\s*)?([^\n\r\0]*)/;

  function Cookie() {}

  function parse(str, options) {
    if (!options || typeof options !== 'object') {
      options = {};
    }
    str = str.trim();

    var firstSemi = str.indexOf(';');
    var pairRe = options.loose ? LOOSE_COOKIE_PAIR : COOKIE_PAIR;
    var result = pairRe.exec(firstSemi === -1 ? str : str.substr(0,firstSemi));

    if (!result) {
      return;
    }

    var c = new Cookie();
    c.key = result[2].trim();
    c.value = result[3].trim();
    return c;
  }

  module.exports = {
    Cookie: Cookie,
    parse: parse
  };
  ```

# The isolating pair. Same exported function applying a pattern to its
# parameter, no tough-cookie API: only the quantifier structure of the
# literal differs. The nested-overlap class is classified by the shared cost
# model and served by the call-site literal-argument route; the
# adjacent-overlap class that is this CVE is invisible to both.

test "rank1864 pair: the nested-overlap quantifier class in the same application is reported"
  lang javascript
  file lib/pair.js
  profile library
  expect VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  module.exports = stripAnsi;
  function stripAnsi(text) {
    return text.replace(/\033\[(\d+)*m/g, '');
  }
  ```

test "rank1864 pair: the adjacent-overlap quantifier class in the same application is not reported"
  lang javascript
  file lib/pair.js
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  module.exports = parsePair;
  function parsePair(str) {
    return str.replace(/^(([^=;]+))\s*=\s*([^\n\r\0]*)/g, '');
  }
  ```
